# Cull common-word/name homographs (separate process — needs a frequency list)

## ==Original Issue / follow-on==

Ticket 15 keeps words that have a proper-name sense. But some are *both* a very
common English word and a (rarer) name, and still surface as noise in the
dictionary: `Ginger` (spice / given name), `Bill` (invoice / William), `Ball`
(sphere / surname), `saint` (holy person). A simple part-of-speech filter cannot
separate these from genuine proper nouns like `Washington` (which also has a
figurative noun sense) — the distinguishing factor is "how common is the
common-word use", i.e. frequency data.

## Plan (separate process)

- Bring in a common-English-word frequency list (e.g. a top-N word list, an
  English wordlist with frequency ranks, or Wiktionary/kaikki frequency data).
- Drop a candidate if its (also-)common-noun sense ranks above some frequency
  threshold AND the proper-name sense is a given name / surname — i.e. it is
  "just a common word that is occasionally a name".
- This correctly keeps people/places whose lowercase form is only a rare
  technical homograph (e.g. `Newton`/`newton` the SI unit, `Bolívar`/`bolívar`
  the currency, `Gilbert`/`gilbert` the CGS unit).
- Emit a config knob (env var, like tickets 13/14) for the frequency cutoff.

